2. The Cognitive Habitat Vision

We model the habitat as eight interacting layers (Figure 1, conceptual):

1. Perception – all sensors, cameras, LIDAR, biomedical monitors, structural health sensors.
2. Adaptive Memory Value Engine (AMVE) – the focus of this paper.
3. Cognitive Memory – episodic, semantic, procedural, spatial, resource, maintenance, scientific stores.
4. Cognitive Decision Layer – mission planning, fault diagnosis, scheduling, resource allocation.
5. Habitat Management – environmental control, life support, power, thermal regulation.
6. Robotic Ecosystem – rovers, drones, humanoid assistants, ISRU machines.
7. Communication – habitat mesh, surface relays, orbital links, delay‑tolerant networking.
8. Mission Intelligence – science campaign planning, long‑term expansion.

AMVE sits at the core, receiving candidate memories from Layers 1, 5, 6 and serving retrieval requests from Layers 4 and 8. Every memory is evaluated by a single, decision‑theoretic value: FMV. The architecture is designed to ensure that the habitat’s collective memory is always oriented toward maximising long‑term mission value.

