6. Impossibility of Static Thresholds

It is tempting to set a fixed threshold \tau and admit a memory if \text{FMV}(m;S) > \tau. We show that this is fundamentally suboptimal.

Observation 1. No universal static threshold \tau can be optimal across all non‑stationary habitat environments.

Justification. Consider two environments differing only in the distribution of candidate memories: in one, all candidates have FMV concentrated on a positive value; in the other, FMV is negative for all candidates. Any fixed threshold will either reject good memories in the first or admit bad ones in the second. More subtly, within a single habitat, the optimal threshold fluctuates as the composition of the store and the mission phase evolve. Therefore, adaptation is mathematically forced. \square

---

7. Adaptive Threshold Controller

We implement a proportional–integral (PI) controller that adjusts the admission threshold to maintain a target memory utilisation. Let K be a soft capacity and \rho_{\text{target}} the target utilisation. The store size at time t is N_t = |S_t|. The threshold \tau_t evolves as

\tau_{t+1} = \tau_t + \kappa \left( \frac{N_t}{K} - \rho_{\text{target}} \right), \qquad \kappa > 0. \tag{2}

The controller is model‑free: it uses only the observed store size, a readily available metric.

Assumption A1. The joint process of candidate arrivals, FMV values, and eviction events is regenerative with finite mean cycle length. The mean store size under a fixed threshold \tau, denoted s(\tau), is a strictly decreasing Lipschitz function.

Theorem 2 (Stability of Adaptive Threshold). Under Assumption A1, for sufficiently small \kappa, the sequence \{\tau_t\} converges almost surely to the unique root \tau^* of s(\tau)/K = \rho_{\text{target}}. Moreover, for any \delta > 0, with probability at least 1-\delta, the tracking error |\tau_t - \tau^*| and the utilisation deviation |N_t/K - \rho_{\text{target}}| become smaller than any prescribed \varepsilon within O(1/\kappa) steps.

Proof. This is a standard two‑timescale stochastic approximation result (Borkar, 2008, Chapter 6). The ODE limit of the threshold update is \dot{\tau} = s(\tau)/K - \rho_{\text{target}}, which has a globally asymptotically stable equilibrium at \tau^* because s(\cdot) is decreasing. Regenerative mixing ensures that the noise satisfies Kushner–Clark conditions, yielding convergence. Finite‑time bounds follow from Kushner & Yin (2003). \square

The controller therefore guarantees that, without any explicit model of the FMV distribution, the admission threshold self‑tunes to maintain the desired store occupancy.

