8. Information‑Theoretic Bound on Immediate Reward Gain

While FMV provides an exact decision rule, computing it exactly is often infeasible. An information‑theoretic upper bound can serve as a computationally cheap pre‑filter. Let Z be the latent task variable. Define the posterior p(z \mid S). The Arimoto‑Rényi conditional mutual information of order 2 between a candidate m and Z given S is

I_2(m; Z \mid S) = -\log \sum_{z} p(z \mid S) \left( \sum_{m'} p(m' \mid z, S)^2 \right)^{1/2}.

This quantity measures the reduction in uncertainty about the task due to observing m, in a way sensitive to the squared Hellinger distance (Sason & Verdú, 2016).

Theorem 3 (Single‑Step Information Bound). If the immediate task reward r(S,\omega) is bounded in [0, R_{\max}], then for any S and m:

r(S \cup \{m\}, \omega) - r(S, \omega) \;\le\; R_{\max} \sqrt{2 \; I_2(m; Z \mid S)}. \tag{3}

Proof. The left‑hand side equals \mathbb{E}_{p_1}[f(z)] - \mathbb{E}_{p_0}[f(z)] for a bounded function f. By Hölder’s inequality and the relationship between total variation and Rényi divergence of order 2 (Verdú, 2015), \|p_1 - p_0\|_{\text{TV}} \le \sqrt{2 D_2(p_1 \| p_0)}, where D_2 is the Rényi divergence. The conditional mutual information I_2(m; Z \mid S) can be expressed as an average of D_2 over the marginal of m. The bound follows. \square

Practical use. Although Theorem 3 bounds only the immediate task reward gain, not the full FMV, it can be employed as a fast screening test: if R_{\max} \sqrt{2 I_2(m; Z \mid S)} \ll c(m), the memory is unlikely to have positive FMV and can be discarded without full critic evaluation. In Gaussian settings, this bound is significantly tighter than the analogous bound based on Shannon mutual information.

