9. Learning FMV from Experience

9.1 Submodular Neural Value Function

We approximate V^* using a neural network \hat{V}_\theta(S) that accepts variable‑sized sets S and outputs a scalar. To respect the submodularity property, we adopt the Submodular Deep Sets architecture (Tschiatschek et al., 2014; Sha & Zhang, 2022): the network first maps each memory element independently to a feature vector, then applies a permutation‑invariant pooling (e.g., sum or max), and finally passes the result through a monotone, concave function. By construction, \hat{V}_\theta(S \cup \{m\}) - \hat{V}_\theta(S) \ge 0 and diminishes as S grows.

Theorem 4 (Approximation Guarantee). Given a target submodular value function V^* that is Lipschitz and defined on a compact domain of bounded set size, there exists a submodular deep set architecture with sufficiently many parameters such that \| \hat{V}_\theta - V^* \|_\infty \le \varepsilon.

Proof. This follows from the universal approximation theorem for permutation‑invariant functions (Zaheer et al., 2017) combined with the fact that submodular functions admit a concave‑max representation (Lovász, 1983). One can approximate the Lovász extension arbitrarily well with a neural network and enforce concavity via concave activation functions. \square

9.2 Learning via Fitted Value Iteration

We train the critic using batch fitted value iteration. At each iteration, a dataset of tuples (S_t, m_t, r_t, S_{t+1}) is collected from the habitat (or a simulator). The target for a state S is

y(S) = \mathbb{E}_m \left[ \max\Big\{ -c(m) + \hat{V}_{\theta}(S \cup \{m\}),\; \hat{V}_\theta(S) \Big\} \right] + \gamma \mathbb{E}_\omega[r(S,\omega)].

The parameters \theta are updated by minimising the squared error \| \hat{V}_\theta(S) - y(S) \|^2, while maintaining submodularity through a projection step or via regularisation. Under standard conditions (Munos & Szepesvári, 2008), the sequence of value functions converges to the best approximation within the function class.

The resulting critic \hat{V}_\theta then defines an approximate FMV: \widehat{\text{FMV}}(m;S) = \hat{V}_\theta(S \cup \{m\}) - \hat{V}_\theta(S) - c(m). The greedy policy based on \widehat{\text{FMV}} is near‑optimal if the approximation error is small.

9.3 Meta‑Learning the Admission Policy

We also consider an end‑to‑end learning approach, where an actor‑critic agent directly optimises the admission policy to maximise cumulative return. Let \pi_\phi(a=1 \mid m,S) be a parametric policy (e.g., sigmoid of \widehat{\text{FMV}}_\phi - \beta_\phi). The performance objective is J(\phi) = \mathbb{E}\big[ \sum \gamma^t r_t \big].

Theorem 5 (Actor‑Critic Convergence). Assume the memory store has a finite capacity K (with eviction of lowest‑strength memories when necessary) so that the state space is finite. If the critic learns on a fast timescale and tracks Q^{\pi_\phi} without bias, and the policy gradient is Lipschitz, then under the standard two‑timescale stochastic approximation analysis (Konda & Tsitsiklis, 2003), the parameters \phi_t converge almost surely to a compact set of stationary points of J.

Proof sketch. The finiteness of the state space implies that the induced Markov chain is uniformly ergodic for any stationary policy. Lipschitz continuity of the score function and bounded rewards ensure that the ODE limit of the policy update is a gradient flow. The convergence follows from Borkar (2008, Chapter 6). \square

This result provides a principled foundation for continuous improvement of the admission policy over the multi‑year mission.

