10. Graceful Forgetting via Continuous Consolidation

Hard eviction (e.g., deleting the oldest or least recently used memory) causes abrupt information loss. We propose a continuous consolidation mechanism inspired by biological memory. Each stored memory i has a strength w_i(t) that evolves as an Ornstein‑Uhlenbeck (OU) process:

dw_i = \eta \big( \widehat{\text{FMV}}_i(t) - \tau_t \big) dt + \sigma \, dW_t, \tag{4}

where \widehat{\text{FMV}}_i(t) is re‑estimated periodically, \tau_t is the current admission threshold, and W_t is a Wiener process. Eviction occurs when w_i falls below a minimum strength w_{\min}.

Theorem 6 (Forgetting Curve). Assume \widehat{\text{FMV}}_i(t) is approximately constant between re‑evaluations with mean \mu. Then the probability that a memory is retained after time t is the survival function of an OU process:

P(\text{retained at } t) = \Phi\!\left( \frac{w_0 + d t - w_{\min}}{\sigma\sqrt{t}} \right) - e^{-2d(w_0 - w_{\min})/\sigma^2} \Phi\!\left( \frac{-w_0 - d t + w_{\min}}{\sigma\sqrt{t}} \right),

where d = \eta(\mu - \tau^*) and \Phi is the standard normal CDF. This function is sigmoidal and converges to zero if d \le 0 or to a positive limit if d > 0.

Proof. The strength process is an OU process with constant drift d and diffusion \sigma. The first‑passage time to w_{\min} follows an inverse Gaussian distribution, and the survival function is as given. \square

This mechanism automatically preserves high‑value memories and gracefully discards low‑value ones, avoiding catastrophic forgetting.

