11. Architectural Specification and Technical Integration

We now embed these mathematical components into a concrete architecture for the eight‑layer habitat (Section 2). AMVE comprises the following core modules:

11.1 FMV Estimator

· Architecture: A Submodular Deep Sets network. Each memory embedding (256‑dimensional vector from Layer 1) is projected via a shared MLP, then aggregated by a permutation‑invariant sum followed by a learnable, element‑wise concave nonlinearity (e.g., softplus with learned exponent) and a final linear layer with non‑negative weights. This structure guarantees that marginal gains are non‑negative and non‑increasing.
· Training: Batch fitted value iteration (Section 9.2) using a target network updated every 100 environment steps. Submodularity is maintained via a projection step onto the cone of monotone submodular set functions, implemented by constraining the Jacobian of the output with respect to set elements.

11.2 Admission Gate

· Decision rule: Accept if \widehat{\text{FMV}}(m;S) > \tau_t.
· Adaptive threshold: Updated by the PI controller (2) with \kappa = 0.01/K. The target utilisation \rho_{\text{target}} is set to 0.85 to leave headroom for bursty arrivals.

11.3 Consolidation Manager

· Strength update: Each memory’s strength evolves according to (4), discretised with step size \Delta t = 1 per hour. Re‑evaluation of \widehat{\text{FMV}} occurs every 24 hours for all stored memories. Eviction is triggered when w_i < w_{\min}.
· Parameter choice: \eta and \sigma are chosen such that a memory with zero FMV decays to w_{\min} in approximately 30 cycles, providing a natural grace period.

11.4 Redundancy Detector

· Operation: Compute the cosine similarity between the embedding of a new candidate and existing memories. If the maximum similarity exceeds 0.95, multiply the candidate’s FMV by (1 - \text{similarity}) before admission, discouraging near‑duplicates.

11.5 Compression Engine

· Schema formation: Periodically, clusters of memories sharing the same semantic label and high similarity are compressed into a single “schema” memory. The schema inherits the maximum strength of the cluster; original items are marked inactive but can be restored if retrieval demand increases (a future extension).

11.6 Retrieval Optimiser

· Context‑aware recall: Given a query from the Decision Layer, the Retrieval Optimiser ranks stored memories by \widehat{\text{FMV}}(m; S_{\text{query}}) \times (\text{query‑embedding similarity}). This uses the same submodular critic to compute the marginal value of each memory for that specific query, thus retrieving only the most relevant and valuable information.

11.7 Integration with Habitat Layers

· Perception (Layer 1): Raw data are encoded by a pre‑trained convolutional backbone into 256‑dimensional embeddings, which serve as candidate memories.
· Cognitive Memory (Layer 3): Physical storage is a distributed graph database with indices by time, location, and semantic tag. Each entry carries its current strength w_i and the latest FMV estimate.
· Decision Layer (Layer 4): All planning and diagnosis tasks receive a ranked list of top‑k memories from the Retrieval Optimiser, drastically reducing search time.
· Robotic Ecosystem (Layer 6): Each robot maintains a cache synchronised with the global store via AMVE. Robot observations are submitted as candidates; AMVE decides global retention.
· Communication (Layer 7): When bandwidth is scarce, memories with highest FMV are transmitted first, solving the prioritisation problem optimally with respect to the global value function.
· Mission Intelligence (Layer 8): Long‑term scientific data are aged according to the consolidation manager, ensuring unique discoveries are never lost.

11.8 Cognitive Health Metrics

AMVE continuously monitors:

· Calibration error: Mean squared difference between predicted \widehat{\text{FMV}} and realised discounted return contribution.
· Retention efficiency: Fraction of evicted memories that were never retrieved during their lifetime.
· Store balance: Moving average of |N_t/K - \rho_{\text{target}}|.
· Redundancy index: Average pairwise cosine similarity.
  If calibration error exceeds a threshold, the critic is flagged for retraining; if balance error grows, the PI learning rate is temporarily increased.

11.9 Computational Budget

The submodular transformer processes sets up to a soft capacity K = 10^6 elements using an efficient O(K \log K) attention implementation. To reduce load, the information‑theoretic bound (3) is used as a pre‑filter: candidates with R_{\max} \sqrt{2 I_2} < c(m) + \tau_t are discarded without critic evaluation. This can eliminate up to 80% of candidates during peak load. The consolidation manager runs as a background thread, re‑evaluating FMV for 1% of the store per cycle.

