12. Related Work

Our work draws on several established bodies of knowledge:

· Submodular optimisation in sensor placement and active learning (Nemhauser et al., 1978; Krause & Guestrin, 2009). We extend these principles to a dynamic memory context.
· Reinforcement learning with set‑valued states (Zaheer et al., 2017; Tschiatschek et al., 2014). Our use of submodular neural architectures is a direct application of recent results in learning set functions.
· Memory‑augmented neural networks and continual learning (Graves et al., 2016; Kirkpatrick et al., 2017). Our OU‑based consolidation provides a mathematically principled alternative to heuristics like Elastic Weight Consolidation.
· Information‑theoretic intrinsic motivation (Still & Precup, 2012). Theorem 3 sharpens the connection between information gain and reward using the Rényi divergence.
· Stochastic approximation for threshold control (Borkar, 2008; Kushner & Yin, 2003). Our PI controller is a direct application of these classical results.

This work uniquely combines these strands into a single, decision‑theoretic memory architecture tailored for autonomous space habitats.

