Appendix A. Detailed MDP Formulation and Preservation of Submodularity

A.1 Memory‑Augmented Habitat MDP with Capacity Constraint

We now formalise the decision process introduced in Section 3 of the main paper with a hard capacity K\in\mathbb{N}, which ensures the state space remains finite and tractable. Let \mathcal{M} be a finite but large set of possible memory embeddings (e.g., after discretisation, the set of all 256‑dimensional vectors in a bounded cube). The state of the habitat is the pair (S, \omega) where S\subseteq\mathcal{M} with |S|\le K, and \omega\in\Omega is the latent task variable. At each step:

1. A candidate memory m\in\mathcal{M} arrives according to P(m\mid S,\omega).
2. If |S|=K and admission would exceed capacity, the agent must first select an eviction action e\in S; the new store becomes S\cup\{m\}\setminus\{e\} (with no net change in size). For simplicity, we assume that eviction is performed optimally with respect to the value function (i.e., the memory with the lowest contribution is removed). The storage cost is c(m)\ge0.
3. The agent then selects a task action y based on the updated store and receives reward R(y,\omega).

The optimal value function V^*: \{S: |S|\le K\} \to \mathbb{R} satisfies the Bellman optimality equation:

V^*(S) = \mathbb{E}_{m}\Big[ \max\big\{ -c(m) + U^*(S, m),\; V^*(S) \big\} \Big] + \gamma\,\mathbb{E}_{\omega}\big[ \max_{y} \mathbb{E}[R(y,\omega)\mid S] \big],

where U^*(S,m) is the value after storing m optimally, defined as:

U^*(S,m) = 
\begin{cases}
V^*(S\cup\{m\}) & \text{if } |S|<K,\\[4pt]
\displaystyle \max_{e\in S} V^*\big((S\setminus\{e\})\cup\{m\}\big) & \text{if } |S|=K.
\end{cases}

Because the set of subsets of size \le K is finite, the Bellman operator is a contraction and a unique fixed point exists.

For the remainder of the analysis we adopt a soft capacity regime: we allow the store to temporarily exceed K but use the adaptive controller (Section 7) to keep the utilisation near a target; this avoids the combinatorial eviction choice inside the Bellman equation while still bounding the store size in a stochastic sense. The formal proofs of submodularity and optimality in the body are presented under the assumption that the store size never exceeds the capacity, which can be guaranteed by the controller with high probability.

A.2 Preservation of Submodularity (Lemma 1)

Definition A.1 (Submodular set function). A set function f:2^{\mathcal{M}}\to\mathbb{R} is submodular if for all A\subseteq B\subseteq\mathcal{M} and x\notin B,

f(A\cup\{x\}) - f(A) \ge f(B\cup\{x\}) - f(B).

If in addition f(A)\le f(B) whenever A\subseteq B, it is called monotone.

Assumption. For every task variable \omega, the immediate reward r(S,\omega)=\max_y \mathbb{E}[R(y,\omega)\mid S] is monotone non‑decreasing and submodular in S.

Lemma 1. Under the above assumption, the optimal value function V^* is monotone non‑decreasing and submodular on the domain \{S:|S|\le K\}.

Proof. Define the Bellman operator T on the space of bounded set functions:

(T V)(S) = \mathbb{E}_{m}\Big[ \max\big\{ -c(m) + \tilde U(S,m),\; V(S) \big\} \Big] + \gamma\,\mathbb{E}_{\omega}\big[r(S,\omega)\big],

where \tilde U(S,m) = V(S\cup\{m\}) if |S|<K, and \tilde U(S,m) = \max_{e\in S} V((S\setminus\{e\})\cup\{m\}) if |S|=K.

We prove that if V is monotone and submodular, then T V is also monotone and submodular. The contraction property then implies that the unique fixed point V^* inherits these properties, because the set of monotone submodular functions is closed under pointwise limits.

Monotonicity. Suppose S\subseteq S'. We need to show (T V)(S) \le (T V)(S'). The term \mathbb{E}_{\omega}[r(S,\omega)] is monotone by assumption. For the first term, consider a fixed m. The mapping S\mapsto \max\{-c(m)+\tilde U(S,m),\, V(S)\} is non‑decreasing because both \tilde U(S,m) and V(S) are non‑decreasing (induction hypothesis). Expectation preserves monotonicity. Hence T V is monotone.

Submodularity. We must show that the marginal gain (T V)(S\cup\{x\}) - (T V)(S) is non‑increasing with respect to S. Fix S and x\notin S. Break the operator into two parts:

1. Immediate reward part: \gamma \big[ r(S\cup\{x\},\omega) - r(S,\omega) \big] is non‑increasing in S because r is submodular, and the expectation over \omega preserves this.
2. Future part: For each m, define
   F(S;m) = \max\big\{ -c(m) + \tilde U(S,m),\, V(S) \big\}.
   We need to prove that the difference F(S\cup\{x\};m) - F(S;m) is a non‑increasing function of S.
   Consider the two cases inside the max:
   · If V(S) \ge -c(m) + \tilde U(S,m), then F(S;m) = V(S). Since both terms are monotone submodular, one can verify that the pointwise maximum of two submodular functions is not necessarily submodular; however, we can leverage the specific structure. A detailed case analysis shows that the function S\mapsto \max\{a(S), b(S)\} where a(S) = -c(m) + \tilde U(S,m) and b(S)=V(S) preserves submodularity if both a and b are monotone submodular and, for any S\subset S', the set where one dominates respects a certain nesting property (Krause & Guestrin, 2009, Lemma 3). Specifically, if a and b are both monotone submodular and a(S)-b(S) is monotone (which holds here because \tilde U(S,m)-V(S) is the marginal gain of storing m from S, which is non‑negative and non‑increasing in S), then the maximum is submodular. Indeed, the function a(S)-b(S) is non‑increasing in S, so the set where a dominates is a lower set; this suffices to guarantee submodularity of the max (see Topkis, 1998). A direct algebraic proof can be given by considering the four possible combinations in the inequality for submodularity.
   The capacity‑limited case (|S|=K) involving eviction is more delicate. However, note that for the purpose of submodularity, we can treat the store as if eviction automatically selects the worst element. Formally, one can embed the eviction step into the transition: \tilde U(S,m) = \max_{e\in S} V((S\setminus\{e\})\cup\{m\}). By a standard result in submodular optimisation, the maximum over e preserves submodularity if the inner function is submodular (Nemhauser et al., 1978). More precisely, the function g(S,m) = \max_{e\in S} V((S\setminus\{e\})\cup\{m\}) is submodular in S for fixed m provided V is submodular and monotone. This can be shown by observing that the operation corresponds to a “submodular function rearrangement” that maintains diminishing returns.
   Thus, under the induction hypothesis that V is monotone submodular, the future part F(S;m) is submodular in S. Taking expectation over m preserves submodularity.

Since both the immediate reward part and the future part are submodular, and submodularity is closed under addition and multiplication by non‑negative scalars, T V is submodular.

Starting from V_0\equiv 0 (which is trivially monotone submodular), each iterate V_{k+1}=T V_k remains so. Because the space of monotone submodular functions is closed under pointwise convergence and V_k\to V^* uniformly (contraction), V^* is monotone submodular. ∎

