Appendix C. Adaptive Threshold Controller: Full Convergence Analysis

C.1 Formal Setup

Let N_t = |S_t| be the store size at time t. The PI controller update is

\tau_{t+1} = \tau_t + \kappa \left( \frac{N_t}{K} - \rho_{\mathrm{target}} \right), \qquad \kappa > 0. \tag{C.1}

The admission decision at time t is: store m_t if \widehat{\mathrm{FMV}}_t(m_t;S_t) > \tau_t, otherwise reject. Eviction (if capacity is exceeded) follows the consolidation manager.

We impose the following regularity assumptions.

Assumption C.1. The joint process of candidate arrivals, approximate FMV values, and eviction events is a regenerative process with a finite mean cycle length. More precisely, there exists an increasing sequence of random times 0 = T_0 < T_1 < T_2 < \dots such that the process over each cycle [T_i, T_{i+1}) is i.i.d., and \mathbb{E}[T_{i+1}-T_i] < \infty, \mathbb{E}[|N_{T_{i+1}}-N_{T_i}|^p] < \infty for some p>1.

Assumption C.2. For a fixed threshold \tau, the long‑term average store size exists and is given by a function s(\tau):

s(\tau) = \lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} N_t \quad \text{(a.s.)}.

Moreover, s(\tau) is Lipschitz continuous and strictly decreasing.

Remark. The decreasing property follows from the fact that a higher threshold makes admission stricter, reducing the number of stored memories on average.

C.2 ODE Approximation

When \kappa is small, the evolution of \tau_t can be approximated by the continuous‑time ODE

\dot{\tau}(t) = \kappa \left( \frac{s(\tau(t))}{K} - \rho_{\mathrm{target}} \right). \tag{C.2}

Because s(\tau) is strictly decreasing, the right‑hand side is strictly decreasing in \tau and has a unique equilibrium \tau^* satisfying s(\tau^*)/K = \rho_{\mathrm{target}}. Linearising around \tau^* yields a stable system: \dot{\tau} \approx \kappa s'(\tau^*)/K \cdot (\tau - \tau^*) with s'(\tau^*) < 0, so the equilibrium is globally asymptotically stable.

C.3 Stochastic Approximation and Almost Sure Convergence

We embed the discrete recursion (C.1) into the standard stochastic approximation framework (Borkar, 2008). Write the update as

\tau_{t+1} = \tau_t + \kappa \big( h(\tau_t) + M_{t+1} \big),

where h(\tau) = s(\tau)/K - \rho_{\mathrm{target}} and M_{t+1} = \frac{N_t}{K} - \frac{s(\tau_t)}{K} is a zero‑mean noise term.

Theorem C.1 (Almost sure convergence). Under Assumptions C.1–C.2 and with a sufficiently small \kappa, the sequence \{\tau_t\} converges almost surely to \tau^*.

Proof. The regeneration property ensures that the process mixes exponentially fast, so the noise sequence has bounded variance and satisfies the Kushner–Clark conditions (Kushner & Yin, 2003). The ODE (C.2) has a unique globally asymptotically stable equilibrium. Application of Borkar (2008, Theorem 2.1 and Corollary 2.2) yields \tau_t \to \tau^* a.s. ∎

C.4 Finite‑Time High‑Probability Bounds

Theorem C.2 (Finite‑time bound). For any \varepsilon > 0 and \delta \in (0,1), there exist \kappa_0>0 and a time horizon T = O(1/\kappa) such that for all \kappa \le \kappa_0,

\mathbb{P}\left( |\tau_T - \tau^*| \le \varepsilon \;\text{and}\; \left|\frac{N_T}{K} - \rho_{\mathrm{target}}\right| \le \varepsilon \right) \ge 1 - \delta.

Proof sketch. Using the Lyapunov function W(\tau) = (\tau - \tau^*)^2, one can derive a recursion for the expected squared error. Under regeneration, a strong mixing bound controls the cross‑terms, leading to an inequality of the form

\mathbb{E}[W(\tau_{t+1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_t] \le W(\tau_t) - c \kappa W(\tau_t) + C \kappa^2,

for some constants c, C > 0. Unrolling this and applying Azuma–Hoeffding or Bernstein’s inequality for martingales gives the exponential tail bound. The required number of steps to reach within \varepsilon is O(1/\kappa). A detailed argument can be found in Kushner & Yin (2003, Chapter 10). ∎

