Appendix D. Information‑Theoretic Bound: Full Derivation

D.1 Rényi Divergence and Rényi Conditional Mutual Information

For discrete distributions P and Q on the same finite set \mathcal{Z}, the Rényi divergence of order \alpha>1 is defined as

D_\alpha(P \,\|\, Q) = \frac{1}{\alpha-1} \log \sum_{z} P(z)^\alpha Q(z)^{1-\alpha}.

For \alpha=2, we have

D_2(P \,\|\, Q) = \log \sum_z \frac{P(z)^2}{Q(z)}.

The conditional Rényi mutual information of order 2 between memory M and task variable Z given store S is

I_2(M; Z \mid S) = \mathbb{E}_{m\sim P_M} \big[ D_2\big( P_{Z\mid S, m} \,\|\, P_{Z\mid S} \big) \big].

An equivalent expression using the joint distribution is

I_2(M;Z\mid S) = -\log \sum_{z} P_{Z\mid S}(z) \Big( \sum_{m} P_{M\mid Z,S}(m)^2 \Big)^{1/2}.

D.2 Relationship to Total Variation and Hellinger Distance

For any two distributions P and Q, the total variation distance satisfies

\|P - Q\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le \sqrt{2\, D_2(P \,\|\, Q)}.

This can be derived from the inequality \|P-Q\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le \sqrt{2\, H^2(P,Q)} where H is the Hellinger distance, and the fact that H^2(P,Q) \le D_2(P\|Q) (Sason & Verdú, 2016, Lemma 1).

D.3 Proof of Theorem 3 (Single‑Step Information Bound)

Theorem 3. If r(S,\omega) \in [0, R_{\max}], then

r(S\cup\{m\},\omega) - r(S,\omega) \le R_{\max} \sqrt{2\, I_2(m; Z \mid S)}.

Proof. Fix S and m. The expected immediate reward given store T is

\mathbb{E}_{\omega\sim P_{Z\mid T}}[ r(T,\omega) ].

Define the distributions P_0 = P_{Z\mid S} and P_1 = P_{Z\mid S\cup\{m\}}. Then

\mathbb{E}_{\omega\sim P_1}[r] - \mathbb{E}_{\omega\sim P_0}[r] = \sum_{z} \big(P_1(z) - P_0(z)\big) r(z) \le R_{\max} \|P_1 - P_0\|_{\mathrm{TV}},

where we treat r(z) as shorthand for r(T,z) with the appropriate T. Using the Rényi divergence bound,

\|P_1 - P_0\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le \sqrt{2\, D_2(P_1 \,\|\, P_0)}.

Now note that P_1 is the posterior given both S and m, so D_2(P_1 \| P_0) is the pointwise Rényi divergence for the observed m. Taking the expectation over m yields exactly I_2(m; Z \mid S). Hence, for the realisation of m,

\|P_1 - P_0\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le \sqrt{2\, D_2(P_1 \| P_0)},

and Jensen’s inequality (concave square root) can be applied after expectation, but the inequality holds pathwise. Therefore,

r(S\cup\{m\},\omega) - r(S,\omega) \le R_{\max} \sqrt{2\, D_2(P_{Z\mid S,m} \,\|\, P_{Z\mid S})}.

Averaging over m gives the bound in terms of I_2. ∎

Remark. This bound applies only to the immediate task reward. The long‑term FMV involves the entire discounted sum of future rewards, which cannot be bounded by single‑step information gain without additional mixing assumptions. Its utility in AMVE is as a fast, computationally light pre‑filter: if the immediate information gain is far below the storage cost, the memory is unlikely to be valuable.

