Appendix G. Graceful Forgetting: Ornstein–Uhlenbeck First‑Passage Time

G.1 Model

Each memory i has a strength w_i(t) evolving according to the SDE

dw_i = \eta (\hat{F}_i - \tau_t) dt + \sigma dW_t,

where \hat{F}_i is the re‑evaluated FMV, assumed constant between re‑evaluations. We treat \tau_t as constant at its equilibrium \tau^* for the purpose of analysis. Thus, for a given memory, the process is an OU process with drift parameter d = \eta(\mu - \tau^*) and diffusion \sigma. Eviction occurs when w_i hits w_{\min} for the first time.

G.2 First‑Passage Time and Survival Function

The first‑passage time T = \inf\{t\ge0: w_i(t) \le w_{\min}\} for an OU process with initial value w_0 > w_{\min} has a known distribution: T follows an inverse Gaussian distribution (or a related truncated form). The survival function P(T > t) = P(\text{retained at } t) is given by (Ricciardi & Sato, 1988):

P(T > t) = \Phi\!\left( \frac{w_0 - w_{\min} + d t}{\sigma\sqrt{t}} \right) - e^{-2d(w_0 - w_{\min})/\sigma^2} \, \Phi\!\left( \frac{-(w_0 - w_{\min}) + d t}{\sigma\sqrt{t}} \right). \tag{G.1}

Proof sketch. The Kolmogorov backward equation for the survival probability can be solved via the method of images. With absorbing boundary at w_{\min}, the density of the process in the absence of absorption is a Gaussian with mean w_0 + d t and variance \sigma^2 t. The absorbing boundary solution is constructed by reflecting the initial condition; the resulting distribution of the hitting time leads to the expression above. Details can be found in standard textbooks on stochastic processes (e.g., Karlin & Taylor, 1981).

G.3 Asymptotic Behaviour

· If d > 0 (mean FMV above threshold), then as t\to\infty, the exponential term vanishes and \Phi(\infty)=1; the survival probability tends to a positive constant: P(\text{retained}) \to 1 - \exp(-2d(w_0 - w_{\min})/\sigma^2) > 0. Thus memories with positive long‑term value are preserved indefinitely with high probability.
· If d \le 0, the survival probability decays to 0 as t\to\infty. The curve is sigmoidal, exhibiting an initial plateau followed by a smooth decline, which avoids catastrophic forgetting.

This analytical expression is used in Section 10 to characterise the forgetting curve.

